/// Reads rows from a SAP HANA table.
///
/// Reads rows from a SAP HANA table. Supports five modes:
///
/// - `bulk`: reads all rows once then the input terminates (wrap it in
///   [`read_until`](https://docs.redpanda.com/redpanda-connect/components/inputs/read_until)
///   with `restart_input: true` for periodic re-reads).
/// - `incrementing`: polls for rows where `incrementing_column` exceeds the last seen
///   value, emitting only net-new rows. Values from an `IDENTITY` column or a sequence
///   are assigned when a row is inserted, not when it commits: with concurrent writers a
///   lower value can commit after a higher one has been read, and that row is never
///   emitted. Use `timestamp+incrementing` with a `timestamp_delay` longer than your
///   longest write transaction when the table has concurrent writers.
/// - `query`: executes a user-supplied SQL statement and emits one message per result
///   row.
/// - `timestamp`: polls for rows where `timestamp_column` falls within `(last_hwm,
///   database_now - timestamp_delay]`. The bound is read from the database clock (see
///   `timestamp_clock`) and the delay absorbs commit lag. The HWM advances to the window
///   bound once the window is fully consumed. Mid-window, a batch checkpoints the
///   timestamp of its last row when the next row has a later one, so a restart re-reads
///   only the rows of the timestamp that was in progress (rows sharing a timestamp cannot
///   be split).
/// - `timestamp+incrementing`: like `timestamp` but orders by `(timestamp_column,
///   incrementing_column)` and resumes after the exact `(timestamp, incrementing)` pair
///   of the last delivered row, so rows sharing a timestamp are neither duplicated nor
///   missed and a restart mid-window continues from the last acknowledged batch rather
///   than the window start.
///
/// ## Metadata
///
/// Messages produced in `bulk`, `incrementing`, `timestamp`, and `timestamp+incrementing`
/// modes carry the following metadata fields (`query` mode attaches none):
///
/// - `table_name`: The HANA table name.
/// - `database_schema`: The configured `schema_name`. Only present when `schema_name` is
///   set.
/// - `schema`: Avro-compatible schema derived from `SYS.TABLE_COLUMNS`, suitable for use
///   with `schema_registry_encode`. Column additions are detected automatically without a
///   pipeline restart. Only present when `schema_name` is configured.
/// - `primary_key_columns`: JSON array of the table's primary-key column names in key
///   order. Only present when `schema_name` is configured and the table has a primary
///   key.
///
/// ## Permissions
///
/// The connecting user needs `SELECT` on the source table, or on the statement's tables
/// in `query` mode. The user also needs read access to system views in these cases:
///
/// - When `schema_name` is set, the input reads `SYS.TABLE_COLUMNS`, `SYS.INDEX_COLUMNS`,
///   and `SYS.INDEXES` to build the `schema` and `primary_key_columns` metadata.
/// - In the `timestamp` and `timestamp+incrementing` modes, the input reads
///   `SYS.TABLE_COLUMNS` (or `SYS.VIEW_COLUMNS` for a view) to check the type of
///   `timestamp_column` when it connects. Without access, the check is skipped and a
///   warning is logged.
/// - When `incrementing_initial_value` is set and no checkpoint exists yet, the input
///   reads `SYS.TABLE_COLUMNS` (or `SYS.VIEW_COLUMNS` for a view) to bind the initial
///   value with the type of `incrementing_column`. Without catalog rows, it test-binds
///   the value against the column (as a number when it parses as one, then as the literal
///   string) and fails to connect when neither binds.
///
/// HANA lists catalog rows for the objects a user holds a privilege on, so `SELECT` on a
/// table or view is enough for these reads. A view has no rows in `SYS.TABLE_COLUMNS`
/// whatever the privileges, so it is read without `schema` and `primary_key_columns`
/// metadata. For other objects the user cannot see in the catalog, grant `CATALOG READ`.
///
/// ## Known limitations
///
/// - Polling only: deleted rows are never seen, and `incrementing` mode never sees
///   updates to existing rows. The timestamp modes see an update only when the
///   application also updates `timestamp_column`.
/// - Rows whose `incrementing_column` or `timestamp_column` is NULL are never emitted.
/// - `timestamp_column` must be a TIMESTAMP, LONGDATE or SECONDDATE column. Change times
///   stored as `DEC(15,0)` UTC timestamps or as separate date and time character columns,
///   as in many SAP application tables, cannot drive the timestamp modes.
/// - `schema` and `primary_key_columns` metadata come from `SYS.TABLE_COLUMNS`, which
///   does not describe views or calculation views, so those are read without schema
///   metadata.
/// - `schema_registry_encode` derives Avro field names from column names. Column names
///   that are not valid Avro names, such as SAP's `/BIC/` namespaced columns, fail to
///   encode; register the schema yourself and rename the columns in a `mapping` instead
///   of using `schema_metadata`. Table names are sanitised automatically.
/// - `bulk` and `query` have no position to resume from: an error part-way through a read
///   restarts it from the first row, re-sending the rows already delivered.
/// - `query` runs its statement once and then the input ends, like `bulk`, and attaches
///   no metadata. Calculation views with input parameters can only be read this way.
/// - The cursor stays open while downstream components apply backpressure. On a busy HANA
///   system a long-open cursor holds back MVCC garbage collection, so index the
///   `incrementing_column` or `timestamp_column` and avoid outputs that stall for long
///   periods. The first poll of a polling mode without an initial value reads the whole
///   table in key order; set `incrementing_initial_value` or `timestamp_initial_value` to
///   skip history you do not need. Raise `fetch_size` (for example to 1000 to 5000) on
///   high-latency links, together with `checkpoint_limit`.
///
/// ## Troubleshooting
///
/// *`checkpoint key ... holds the position of ...` on connect.* Two `sap_hana` inputs
/// share a `checkpoint_cache` and `checkpoint_cache_key`. Give each input its own
/// `checkpoint_cache_key`. The error also appears when `schema_name` or `table` changed
/// for an existing input: the stored position is for the old reference, so delete the key
/// to start over or set `incrementing_initial_value`/`timestamp_initial_value` under a
/// new key.
///
/// *Cannot connect to SAP HANA Cloud.* HANA Cloud accepts TLS connections only, on port
/// 443. go-hdb enables TLS when a TLS parameter is present, so add `TLSServerName=<host>`
/// to the DSN (and `TLSRootCAFile=<path>` if the CA is not in the system trust store).
///
/// *`schema` and `primary_key_columns` metadata are missing.* The input reads them from
/// `SYS.TABLE_COLUMNS` and `SYS.INDEX_COLUMNS`; when that read fails it logs a warning
/// and carries on without them. Views never have them. For a table, check the warning in
/// the logs, grant the connecting user `SELECT` on the table (or `CATALOG READ`), and
/// make sure `schema_name` is set.
///
/// *"invalid table name" or "invalid column name" for a table that exists.* Identifiers
/// in this configuration are quoted when sent to HANA, so they must match the catalog's
/// case exactly. HANA upper-cases identifiers that were created without quotes: a table
/// created as `create table orders` is `ORDERS` in the catalog and must be configured as
/// such.
///
/// *Batches arrive one at a time.* `fetch_size` larger than `checkpoint_limit` means a
/// batch cannot be handed on until the previous one is acknowledged (a warning is logged
/// at startup). Raise `checkpoint_limit` or lower `fetch_size`.
///
/// *Rows written around the time of a poll are missing in timestamp modes.* The poll
/// window ends at the database clock minus `timestamp_delay`; a row whose timestamp was
/// assigned before that bound but committed after the poll is never seen. Set
/// `timestamp_delay` longer than your longest write transaction, and pick the
/// `timestamp_clock` that matches how the column is populated.
///
/// Status: stable
module com.redpanda.connect.inputs.SapHana
extends "../Input.pkl"

